# a-wreck-left-where-it-fell — plan 15 C3, the control.
#
# A base with ONE way in: a wall across a five-hex band with a single
# gate hex.  A crew member stands in the gate, so it is a blocker with
# nowhere for the wave to go round (plan 13 V5) — it holds the wave for
# ten seconds, is destroyed, and the wreck lies in the gate while the
# wave walks over it to the core.
#
# ⚠ **The gate is what makes this base measurable at all.**  A base
# whose approach is a one-hex CORRIDOR caps the drain at the one or two
# enemies that fit beside the core, so its clock reads the same for six
# attackers as for two and cannot see anything the crew does — measured
# while building this, and the same saturation trap plan 14 H2 § 2 hit
# from the other side.  Here the wave funnels (so a helper can be lost)
# and then spreads (so the drain scales).
#
# Nobody fetches the wreck.  `a-wreck-fetched-under-fire.keys` is the
# same file with the retrieval in it, and § C3 of the plan is the two
# clocks side by side.

echo a wreck left where it fell: nobody goes back for it

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 18 0
marker 18 0 spawn 3

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -3 -2 20 -2
drag -3 -1 20 -1
drag -3 0 20 0
drag -3 1 20 1
drag -3 2 20 2
count painted 120 120

# The wall, with (6, 0) left open — the gate.
palette 9
click 6 -2
click 6 -1
click 6 1
click 6 2
kind 6 0 grass
kind 6 1 wall

# The crew member stands in the gate; the player waits at the core.
crew 6 0
helper 0 6 0
roster 1 1
park 0 0

wave 8
count alive 8 8
wallet 200.0 200.0

fall 800
ticks 80 90

# It was lost, and it is still lying in the gate.
roster 0 0
cargo 1 1
helper 0 6 0

snap c3_a_wreck_left_where_it_fell
